      { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Enter weight and height", "text": "Choose Metric (kg and cm) or Imperial (lbs, feet, inches), then enter weight and height. Results update instantly." },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Read the percentile", "text": "The BMI value, percentile, and category appear immediately. For teens, the 85th percentile is the overweight threshold and the 95th is the obese threshold. At age 20, switch to fixed adult categories." }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is a healthy BMI for a teenager?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A healthy BMI for a teenager is between the 5th and 85th percentile for their age and gender, based on CDC 2000 growth charts. For example, a healthy BMI for a 15-year-old boy is approximately 16.8 to 23.6, and for a 15-year-old girl approximately 16.4 to 24.4. Because normal BMI ranges change every year during adolescence, percentile-based interpretation is more accurate than fixed adult thresholds." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is a normal BMI for a 15-year-old?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "For a 15-year-old boy, healthy weight (5th to 85th percentile) corresponds to a BMI of approximately 16.8 to 23.6. For a 15-year-old girl it is approximately 16.4 to 24.4. The 50th percentile (median BMI) is approximately 20.2 for boys and 20.2 for girls at age 15." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "At what BMI is a teenager overweight?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A teenager is overweight when their BMI-for-age reaches the 85th percentile for their age and gender. This threshold changes at every age. For a 15-year-old boy it is approximately BMI 23.6; for a 15-year-old girl approximately BMI 24.4. At or above the 95th percentile is obese." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does puberty affect BMI in teenagers?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, significantly. During female puberty, estrogen drives an increase in body fat, particularly in the hips, thighs, and breasts - this is biologically normal and healthy. During male puberty, testosterone drives rapid muscle mass and bone density gains, which increases weight without adding fat. The same BMI in a 16-year-old boy and girl therefore represents very different body compositions. A BMI that appears elevated in a teenage boy may largely reflect muscle gain rather than excess fat." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "When does a teenager switch to adult BMI categories?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "At age 20, adult fixed-category thresholds apply: BMI below 18.5 is Underweight, 18.5–24.9 is Normal weight, 25.0–29.9 is Overweight, and 30.0 and above is Obese. Before age 20, percentile-based BMI-for-age is used because normal BMI increases with age throughout adolescence, and applying adult cutoffs would misclassify many healthy older teens." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is BMI accurate for athletic teenagers?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "BMI is less accurate for athletic teens because it cannot distinguish muscle from fat. A teenage boy who lifts weights or plays football may have a BMI above the 85th percentile with low body fat, because his muscle mass is above average for his age. In these cases, a clinical assessment or body fat measurement is more informative than BMI alone." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I worry if my teenager has a high BMI?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A high BMI percentile warrants attention but not alarm. The next step is to speak with a pediatrician, who can assess body composition in context - considering growth trends, physical activity, diet, family history, and the teen's own wellbeing. For adolescents, approaching weight as a health topic (not an appearance issue) is clinically important to avoid triggering disordered eating or poor body image." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can talking about BMI cause eating disorders in teens?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Framing weight purely as an appearance issue or using stigmatizing language can contribute to poor body image and disordered eating risk in teens. The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends focusing on health behaviors (nutrition, activity, sleep) rather than weight or BMI numbers when discussing weight with teenagers. BMI is a screening tool, not a value judgment." }
      }
    ]
  }
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  title="BMI Calculator for Teens"
  metaTitle="BMI Calculator for Teens | BMI-for-Age Percentile (Ages 13–19) ᐅ"
  description="Free BMI calculator for teenagers aged 13–19. Uses CDC 2000 growth charts to show BMI-for-age percentile and weight category. Covers puberty's effect on BMI, athletic teens, and the transition to adult categories at 20."
  canonical="https://calculations.tools/health/bmi-calculator/teens"
  tocItems={tocItems}
  schema={schema}
  embedSlug="bmi-calculator"
  sources={sources}
  ogFormula="BMI-for-age percentile: BMI = weight / height², plotted on CDC chart"
  ogExample="15-year-old girl, 55 kg, 1.63 m: BMI 20.7 = 60th percentile"
>
  <div slot="calculator">
    <div id="bmi-k-container" class="calculator-container bmi-container" data-bmi-mode="teens">
      <div class="calculator-header">
        <h2>Teen BMI Calculator (Ages 13–19)</h2>
        <p>Uses CDC 2000 growth chart percentiles</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bmi-unit-toggle">
        <button class="bmi-k-sex-btn bmi-unit-btn active" data-sex="M">Boy</button>
        <button class="bmi-k-sex-btn bmi-unit-btn" data-sex="F">Girl</button>
      </div>
      <div class="bmi-k-age-row">
        <label class="bmi-label">Age (13–19 years)</label>
        <div class="bmi-k-age-inputs">
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-age-yr" class="bmi-input" min="13" max="19" step="1" placeholder="15" />
            <span class="bmi-unit-tag">yr</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-age-mo" class="bmi-input" min="0" max="11" step="1" placeholder="0" />
            <span class="bmi-unit-tag">mo</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bmi-unit-toggle">
        <button class="bmi-k-unit-btn bmi-unit-btn active" data-unit="metric">Metric</button>
        <button class="bmi-k-unit-btn bmi-unit-btn" data-unit="imperial">Imperial</button>
      </div>
      <div id="bmi-k-metric" class="bmi-inputs active">
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="bmi-k-weight-kg">Weight</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-weight-kg" class="bmi-input" min="20" max="200" step="0.1" placeholder="60" />
            <span class="bmi-unit-tag">kg</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="bmi-k-height-cm">Height</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-height-cm" class="bmi-input" min="100" max="220" step="1" placeholder="165" />
            <span class="bmi-unit-tag">cm</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="bmi-k-imperial" class="bmi-inputs">
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="bmi-k-weight-lb">Weight</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-weight-lb" class="bmi-input" min="44" max="440" step="0.5" placeholder="132" />
            <span class="bmi-unit-tag">lbs</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label">Height</label>
          <div class="bmi-ftin">
            <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
              <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-height-ft" class="bmi-input" min="3" max="7" step="1" placeholder="5" />
              <span class="bmi-unit-tag">ft</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
              <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="bmi-k-height-in" class="bmi-input" min="0" max="11" step="1" placeholder="5" />
              <span class="bmi-unit-tag">in</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="bmi-k-redirect" class="bmi-k-redirect hidden"></div>
      <div id="bmi-k-result" class="bmi-result hidden">
        <div class="bmi-value-row">
          <div>
            <div class="bmi-value" id="bmi-k-value">-</div>
            <div class="bmi-k-percentile" id="bmi-k-percentile">-</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bmi-category-badge" id="bmi-k-category-badge">-</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-scale">
          <div class="bmi-scale-bar">
            <div class="bmi-seg bmi-seg-underweight" style="width:5%"></div>
            <div class="bmi-seg bmi-seg-normal"      style="width:80%"></div>
            <div class="bmi-seg bmi-seg-overweight"  style="width:10%"></div>
            <div class="bmi-seg bmi-seg-obese"       style="width:5%"></div>
            <div class="bmi-indicator" id="bmi-k-indicator"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bmi-scale-labels bmi-k-scale-labels">
            <span style="left:0%">0</span>
            <span style="left:5%">5th</span>
            <span style="left:85%">85th</span>
            <span style="left:95%">95th</span>
            <span style="left:100%">100th</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-details">
          <div class="bmi-detail" id="bmi-k-healthy-range"></div>
          <div class="bmi-detail bmi-detail-hint" id="bmi-k-next-category"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <script src="/js/bmi-kids-calculator.js" is:inline></script>
  <HealthDisclaimer />
  </div>
  <div slot="content">
    <div class="seo-container">
      <div class="answer-box">
        <div class="answer-box-label">Teen BMI at a glance</div>
        <p>Teen BMI is interpreted using age- and gender-specific percentiles, not fixed adult thresholds. A healthy BMI for a teenager is between the <strong>5th and 85th percentile</strong> for their age and gender.</p>
        <p>At or above the 85th percentile is Overweight; at or above the 95th percentile is Obese. Puberty changes what BMI means - the same number looks different in a teenage girl adding healthy body fat and a teenage boy building muscle.</p>
        <p>At <strong>age 20, adult fixed categories</strong> apply. This calculator uses the CDC 2000 LMS method. For children aged 2–12, use the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator/kids">BMI Calculator for Kids</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="answer-box">
        <div class="answer-box-label">Key takeaways</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Teen BMI is measured as a <a href="/glossary/bmi-percentile">BMI-for-age percentile</a>, not a fixed number - the healthy range shifts every year during adolescence because <a href="/glossary/bmi">normal BMI</a> rises with growth.</li>
          <li>The CDC defines <strong>Overweight</strong> as at or above the 85th percentile and <strong>Obese</strong> as at or above the 95th percentile for age and gender.</li>
          <li>Girls naturally gain 6-10% body fat during puberty from estrogen; this is healthy and raises BMI without adding excess fat. Boys gain muscle and bone mass from testosterone, which also raises BMI without adding fat.</li>
          <li>BMI misclassification is most common in teenage boys in strength sports - a muscular athlete may land above the 85th percentile with low body fat.</li>
          <li>At age 20, adult fixed thresholds replace percentiles. For most teens in a healthy percentile range, the transition is seamless - their BMI at the 50th percentile sits squarely in the adult Normal range.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use our teenage BMI calculator</h2>
      <p>Select <strong>Boy</strong> or <strong>Girl</strong>, enter the teen's age in years and months (13–19), then enter weight and height in your preferred units - the calculator supports both <a href="/glossary/metric-system">metric</a> and <a href="/glossary/imperial-system">imperial</a> systems. The calculator shows BMI, percentile, weight category, and the <a href="/health/ideal-weight-calculator">healthy weight range</a> for that exact age and gender.</p>
      <p>If the age entered is outside the 13–19 range, the calculator suggests either the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator/kids">BMI calculator for kids</a> or the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator for adults</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="what-is-bmi-for-age">What is BMI-for-age for teenagers?</h2>
      <p><a href="/glossary/bmi-percentile">BMI-for-age</a> uses the same formula as adult BMI - weight in kg divided by height in m squared - but interprets the result as a percentile compared to a reference population of the same age and gender, using the CDC 2000 growth charts.</p>
      <p>Fixed adult cutoffs like 25 (overweight) and 30 (obese) are not used for teenagers because normal BMI increases steadily through adolescence.</p>
      <p>A 19-year-old is likely to have a BMI between 19 and 23 if their weight is healthy - close to adult ranges. A 13-year-old's healthy range is lower. Applying adult cutoffs to a 13-year-old would classify most healthy young adolescents correctly only by coincidence.</p>
      <h3 id="explained-to-beginner">BMI percentile explained to a beginner</h3>
      <p>Think of it like height-for-age. A 6-year-old who is 4 feet tall is very tall for their age - but a 16-year-old at the same 4 feet would be very short. The same measurement means something completely different depending on how old you are.</p>
      <p><strong>BMI-for-age works the same way:</strong> a BMI of 20 is right at the 50th percentile for a 13-year-old boy (perfectly average) but is below the 40th percentile for a 17-year-old boy (slightly below average). The raw number alone tells you almost nothing.</p>
      <p>The percentile tells you where a teen's BMI sits compared to all other teens of the same age and gender in the CDC reference population. A result at the 70th percentile means 70% of same-age peers have a lower BMI - and 30% have a higher one. The healthy window (5th to 85th percentile) is intentionally wide, because the "right" BMI grows every year as teenagers grow into their bodies.</p>
      <h3>The Body Mass Index formula</h3>
      <Formula expr={f.metric} />
      <Formula expr={f.imperial} />
      <h2 id="categories">BMI categories for teenagers</h2>
      <p>Teen BMI categories use percentile cutoffs, not fixed numbers. The CDC defines four categories based on where a teen's BMI falls relative to same-age, same-gender peers in the 2000 reference population.</p>
      <p>Two key thresholds to know: the <strong>85th percentile</strong> (Overweight) and the <strong>95th percentile</strong> (Obese). Both are higher absolute BMI values for older teens than younger ones - the cutoff rises every year as normal BMI rises with age.</p>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Category</th><th>BMI percentile</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Underweight</td>
              <td>Below 5th</td>
              <td>BMI lower than 95% of same-age peers; may indicate inadequate nutrition, disordered eating, or underlying condition</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Healthy weight</td>
              <td>5th to below 85th</td>
              <td>Within the expected range for age and gender; lowest risk of weight-related complications</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Overweight</td>
              <td>85th to below 95th</td>
              <td>Above 85–94% of peers; elevated risk - especially if BMI percentile is rising or other metabolic risk factors are present</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Obese</td>
              <td>95th and above</td>
              <td>Above 95%+ of peers; substantially elevated risk of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, sleep apnea, and orthopedic stress</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2 id="bmi-chart-by-age">BMI-for-age reference chart: ages 13–19</h2>
      <p>The tables below show BMI at the 5th, 50th, 85th, and 95th percentiles for boys and girls at ages 13–19, based on CDC 2000 LMS parameters. These define the category boundaries at each age.</p>
      <h3>BMI for teenage boys (ages 13–19)</h3>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Age</th>
              <th>5th %ile (Underweight)</th>
              <th>50th %ile (Median)</th>
              <th>85th %ile (Overweight)</th>
              <th>95th %ile (Obese)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>13 yr</td><td>15.7</td><td>18.8</td><td>22.6</td><td>25.6</td></tr>
            <tr><td>14 yr</td><td>16.8</td><td>19.6</td><td>23.5</td><td>26.5</td></tr>
            <tr><td>15 yr</td><td>16.8</td><td>20.2</td><td>23.6</td><td>27.2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16 yr</td><td>18.1</td><td>20.8</td><td>25.0</td><td>28.3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>17 yr</td><td>18.7</td><td>21.3</td><td>25.7</td><td>29.1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>18 yr</td><td>19.1</td><td>21.7</td><td>26.3</td><td>29.9</td></tr>
            <tr><td>19 yr</td><td>19.4</td><td>22.2</td><td>26.9</td><td>30.5</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>BMI for teenage girls (ages 13–19)</h3>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Age</th>
              <th>5th %ile (Underweight)</th>
